  "reasoning": "The target molecule contains a biaryl bond connecting a benzoate core (substituted with a methyl ester and an amino group) to a 4-chlorophenyl ring. Under the context of C-C bond formation, the most strategic disconnection is at this inter-ring bond. Breaking it yields two aromatic fragments. To support a typical cross-coupling mechanism (e.g., Suzuki-Miyaura or Ullmann), one fragment must carry a halogen leaving group. Placing a bromine at the connection site of the chlorophenyl ring gives 1-bromo-4-chlorobenzene. The complementary fragment is the aminobenzoate ester, represented as COC(=O)c1ccc(N)cc1. These precursors logically recombine via Pd-catalyzed cross-coupling to yield the target biaryl.",
  "final_answer": "COC(=O)c1ccc(N)cc1.Clc1ccc(Br)cc1"
}

